exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("1 Chronicles", """
1|The book opens with a family line from Adam to Abraham and his sons, and lists the descendants of Esau and the kings of Edom.
2|This chapter lists the twelve sons of Israel and the descendants of Judah, including David's family and his ancestors Boaz and Jesse.
3|David's sons and the later kings of Judah are listed, down to the exile.
4|More families of Judah are listed, along with Jabez, who prays that God will bless him, and the descendants of Simeon.
5|The descendants of Reuben, Gad and the half-tribe of Manasseh are listed. These tribes lost their land to Assyria because they were unfaithful.
6|The Levites and priests are listed, including Aaron's line and the singers David appointed.
7|The families of Issachar, Benjamin, Naphtali, Manasseh, Ephraim and Asher are listed.
8|More family lines of Benjamin are given, including Saul's family.
9|The families who returned from Babylon to Jerusalem are listed, along with the priests and gatekeepers. Saul's family line is repeated.
10|The Philistines defeat Israel, and Saul and his sons die on Mount Gilboa. The writer says Saul died because he was unfaithful to God.
11|All Israel makes David king, and he takes Jerusalem. His mighty men are listed, along with their brave deeds.
12|Warriors from many tribes join David, ready to make him king. They come with one purpose.
13|David tries to bring the ark to Jerusalem on a cart. Uzzah touches it and dies, and David leaves the ark at the house of Obed-edom.
14|David builds a palace and has children, and with God's help defeats the Philistines twice.
15|David prepares a place for the ark and has the Levites carry it as the law requires. The people bring it up to Jerusalem with music and celebration.
16|David puts the ark in its tent and gives thanks in a psalm of praise. He appoints Levites to serve before the ark.
17|David wants to build a temple, but God says David's son will build it. God promises David a house and a kingdom that will last forever. David responds with thanks.
18|David defeats the Philistines, Moab, Aram and Edom, and sets up his officials.
19|The king of Ammon humiliates David's messengers. Joab and Abishai lead Israel to victory over Ammon and Aram.
20|Joab conquers Rabbah. David and his men defeat several Philistine giants.
21|Satan prompts David to count Israel. God sends a plague, and David sees an angel with a sword over Jerusalem. He buys Ornan's threshing floor and offers a sacrifice.
22|David gathers materials for the temple and tells Solomon to build it. He says Solomon will have peace.
23|David makes Solomon king, and sets the duties of the Levites for the temple.
24|David divides the priests into twenty-four groups, each serving in turn.
25|David sets apart musicians and singers from the Levites to serve in the temple with harps, lyres and cymbals.
26|The temple gatekeepers and the keepers of the treasuries are named.
27|David's army officers, tribal leaders and royal officials are listed.
28|David tells the leaders that Solomon will build the temple. He gives Solomon the plans and tells him to serve God with a whole heart.
29|David and the leaders give gifts for the temple. David prays, praising God: everything comes from you. Solomon is crowned, and David dies after forty years as king.
""")
B("2 Chronicles", """
1|Solomon goes to Gibeon to sacrifice, and God offers him anything. Solomon asks for wisdom and God also gives him riches. His wealth grows.
2|Solomon prepares to build the temple. He asks King Hiram of Tyre for cedar wood and skilled workers.
3|Solomon builds the temple on Mount Moriah in Jerusalem. The chapter describes its size and the two bronze pillars.
4|Solomon makes the bronze altar, the Sea, the ten basins and other temple furnishings.
5|The ark is brought into the temple. When the priests come out, a cloud fills the building with the glory of the Lord.
6|Solomon blesses the people and prays a dedication prayer, asking God to hear his people when they pray toward the temple.
7|Fire comes down from heaven and consumes the offerings. God appears to Solomon and promises that if his people humble themselves and pray, he will hear and heal their land.
8|Solomon finishes his other building projects and puts in place the temple worship as David had ordered.
9|The queen of Sheba visits and is amazed by Solomon's wisdom and wealth. Solomon reigns forty years and dies.
10|Rehoboam answers the people harshly and ten tribes break away to follow Jeroboam. Judah and Benjamin stay with Rehoboam.
11|Rehoboam builds up cities for defense. Priests and Levites leave Israel and move to Judah. Rehoboam has many wives and children.
12|Rehoboam abandons the law, and Shishak of Egypt attacks Jerusalem. Rehoboam humbles himself, and God spares the city.
13|Abijah of Judah defeats Jeroboam of Israel in battle, because Judah relies on God.
14|Asa becomes king and removes idols. When Ethiopia attacks with a huge army, he prays and God gives Judah the victory.
15|The prophet Azariah tells Asa to seek the Lord. Asa leads Judah in a covenant and removes his grandmother from being queen mother for her idol.
16|Asa relies on Aram instead of God and the prophet Hanani rebukes him. Asa dies with a foot disease and does not seek the Lord.
17|Jehoshaphat becomes king and follows the Lord. He sends teachers through Judah, and the neighbors respect him.
18|Jehoshaphat joins Ahab in a war against Aram. The prophet Micaiah warns them and Ahab is killed in battle.
19|Jehoshaphat appoints judges across Judah and tells them to judge for the Lord and not for people.
20|Moab and Ammon invade Judah. Jehoshaphat prays, and singers go out ahead of the army praising God, and God defeats the enemy.
21|Jehoram of Judah kills his brothers and marries Ahab's daughter. He dies of a painful disease.
22|Ahaziah reigns one year and is killed by Jehu. His mother Athaliah kills the royal family, except Joash, who is hidden.
23|The priest Jehoiada crowns Joash and has Athaliah killed. The people renew the covenant.
24|Joash repairs the temple. After Jehoiada dies, Joash turns to idols and has Jehoiada's son Zechariah killed. Joash is later killed by his own servants.
25|Amaziah defeats Edom but worships its gods. He then challenges Israel and is defeated.
26|Uzziah is a strong king, but he becomes proud and enters the temple to burn incense. He is struck with a skin disease.
27|Jotham reigns sixteen years, builds up the land and does what is right.
28|Ahaz worships idols and sacrifices his sons. Judah suffers defeat. He strips the temple and appeals to Assyria.
29|Hezekiah reopens and cleans the temple and restores worship. The people celebrate with sacrifices and music.
30|Hezekiah invites all Israel to keep the Passover in Jerusalem. Many come and God hears Hezekiah's prayer for those who are not clean.
31|The people destroy idols and give their tithes. Hezekiah organizes the priests and Levites.
32|Sennacherib of Assyria invades. Hezekiah and Isaiah pray, and an angel destroys the Assyrian army. Hezekiah becomes ill and recovers.
33|Manasseh does great evil in Judah. In exile in Babylon he humbles himself, and God brings him back. His son Amon is killed.
34|Josiah seeks the Lord, removes idols and repairs the temple. The book of the law is found, and Josiah renews the covenant.
35|Josiah celebrates a great Passover. He is killed at Megiddo, fighting Pharaoh Neco, and Jeremiah laments for him.
36|The last kings of Judah do evil, and Babylon burns Jerusalem and takes the people to exile for seventy years. The book ends as Cyrus of Persia announces that the Lord's people may go back and rebuild the temple.
""")
B("Ezra", """
1|Cyrus, king of Persia, announces that God has told him to rebuild the temple in Jerusalem. He allows the exiles to go home and returns the temple vessels.
2|This chapter lists the families who returned from Babylon with Zerubbabel, about 42,000 people.
3|The returned exiles rebuild the altar and keep the Feast of Booths. They begin laying the foundation of the temple, and some weep while others shout for joy.
4|The neighbors offer to help build and are refused, and then try to stop the work. The building stops for years.
5|The prophets Haggai and Zechariah encourage the people to restart the temple. The governor Tattenai asks Persia whether they have permission.
6|King Darius finds Cyrus's decree and orders that the work go on. The temple is finished and dedicated, and the people keep Passover.
7|Ezra, a priest and scribe, comes to Jerusalem in the reign of Artaxerxes with the king's letter. He has set his heart to study the law of the Lord and teach it.
8|The family heads who travel with Ezra are listed. They fast and pray for safe travel and carry gold and silver to the temple without an escort.
9|Ezra learns that many Israelites have married the people around them and followed their customs. He tears his clothes and prays in shame.
10|The people weep and agree to put away their foreign wives. Leaders are appointed to settle each case, and the guilty are listed.
""")
B("Nehemiah", """
1|Nehemiah, cupbearer to the Persian king, hears that Jerusalem's walls are broken. He weeps, fasts and prays, confessing Israel's sin and asking God for favor.
2|The king sees Nehemiah's sadness and sends him to Jerusalem with letters. Nehemiah inspects the walls at night and tells the people: let us rise up and build.
3|The people repair the wall, each group taking one section. The names of the builders are listed.
4|Sanballat and Tobiah mock the builders and plot to attack. Nehemiah sets guards, and the workers build with a tool in one hand and a weapon in the other.
5|The poor complain that they are losing land and children to debt. Nehemiah rebukes the nobles and has them return what they took.
6|The enemies try to scare Nehemiah and lure him away. The wall is finished in fifty-two days.
7|Nehemiah puts gatekeepers in place and appoints rulers over Jerusalem. He finds and records the list of those who first returned.
8|Ezra reads the law aloud from morning to noon, and the Levites explain it. The people weep, and are told to rejoice. They celebrate the Feast of Booths.
9|The people fast and confess their sins. The Levites pray a long prayer remembering what God has done for Israel and how they have failed.
10|The leaders sign a covenant to follow God's law, not to intermarry, to keep the Sabbath and to support the temple.
11|The leaders live in Jerusalem, and one out of ten of the people is chosen to join them. The towns where others live are listed.
12|The priests and Levites are listed. The wall is dedicated with two great choirs, thanksgiving and sacrifices.
13|Nehemiah returns to Jerusalem after a time away and finds that the people have broken their promises. He cleans the temple, enforces the Sabbath and puts things in order.
""")
B("Tobit", """
1|Tobit, an Israelite taken captive to Nineveh, stays faithful to God. He gives alms and secretly buries the bodies of his people.
2|After burying a man, Tobit is blinded when sparrow droppings fall in his eyes. His wife Anna works to support him, and he becomes bitter.
3|Tobit and Sarah, a young woman in Media whose seven husbands were killed by a demon, each pray to die. God hears both and sends the angel Raphael to help.
4|Tobit tells his son Tobias to honor his mother, give alms, marry within the family and pay a man in Media the money he left there.
5|Tobias looks for a guide to Media and finds Raphael, who says he is a relative named Azarias. The two set off with Tobias's dog.
6|A large fish attacks Tobias at the Tigris. Raphael tells him to keep its heart, liver and gall. He says they will be used to drive off a demon and to heal Tobit's eyes.
7|Tobias and Raphael arrive at the house of Raguel, who is Sarah's father. Tobias asks for Sarah in marriage, and Raguel agrees.
8|On the wedding night Tobias burns the heart and liver, and the demon Asmodeus flees. Tobias and Sarah pray together. Raguel fills in the grave he had dug in case Tobias died.
9|Raphael travels to collect the money Tobit left, and brings the man back to the wedding feast.
10|Tobit and Anna worry when their son does not return. Tobias asks to leave, and Raguel sends them off with half his property.
11|Tobias and Raphael return. Tobias applies the fish's gall to his father's eyes, and Tobit can see again. Tobit blesses God.
12|Raphael tells them to praise God and give alms, and reveals he is an angel, one of the seven who stand before God. He disappears.
13|Tobit sings a hymn of praise to God, who punishes and has mercy. He looks ahead to the rebuilding of Jerusalem.
14|Tobit tells his son to leave Nineveh before it is destroyed. Tobit dies at an old age, and Tobias returns to Media and also lives a long life.
""")
B("Judith", """
1|Nebuchadnezzar, who reigns in Nineveh, makes war on Arphaxad, king of the Medes, and wins. Many nations had refused to help him.
2|Nebuchadnezzar sends his general Holofernes with a great army to punish those who did not help him.
3|Many nations surrender to Holofernes, but they are destroyed anyway. The people of Israel are afraid.
4|The Israelites prepare to defend their mountain passes. They fast, pray and put sackcloth on the altar, and the high priest Joakim leads them.
5|Holofernes asks about the Israelites. Achior, an Ammonite leader, tells their history and says they can only be defeated if they have sinned against God.
6|Holofernes is angry and sends Achior to Bethulia to share their fate. The Israelites take him in.
7|Holofernes besieges Bethulia and cuts off its water supply. The people want to surrender, and their leader Uzziah asks them to wait five more days.
8|Judith, a wise and beautiful widow, rebukes the leaders for testing God. She says she will do something that will be remembered for generations.
9|Judith prays to God for help. She asks him to give her a persuasive tongue and to strike down the enemy by a woman's hand.
10|Judith dresses beautifully and goes with her maid to the enemy camp. She tells the guards she has information for Holofernes, and she is taken to him.
11|Judith tells Holofernes that Israel will fall only when they sin. She says she will tell him when it happens, and he is impressed.
12|Holofernes invites Judith to a banquet. He drinks too much and is left alone with her in his tent.
13|Judith prays and cuts off Holofernes's head. She and her maid carry it back to Bethulia, and the people praise God.
14|Judith tells the Israelites to hang the head on the wall and attack at dawn. When the Assyrians find Holofernes dead, they panic. Achior believes in God and is circumcised.
15|The Israelites chase the fleeing Assyrians and take great plunder. Joakim and the elders come from Jerusalem to bless Judith.
16|Judith sings a song of praise to God. She lives a long life, refuses to remarry and is mourned by all Israel when she dies at 105.
""")
B("Esther", """
1|King Ahasuerus of Persia gives a six-month feast. When Queen Vashti refuses to appear before the guests, he removes her as queen.
2|Esther, a Jewish orphan raised by her cousin Mordecai, is chosen as the new queen. Mordecai overhears a plot to kill the king and tells Esther, who warns the king.
3|The king promotes Haman, who wants everyone to bow to him. Mordecai refuses, so Haman plots to destroy all the Jews and gets the king's decree.
4|Mordecai and the Jews mourn. Mordecai tells Esther that she must plead with the king. He says perhaps she has come to the kingdom for such a time as this.
5|Esther fasts and goes before the king, who welcomes her. She invites the king and Haman to a banquet. Haman builds a gallows for Mordecai.
6|The king cannot sleep and has the record read. He learns that Mordecai was never rewarded, and tells Haman to honor Mordecai publicly.
7|At the second banquet Esther reveals that she is a Jew and that Haman plans to kill her people. The king has Haman hanged on his own gallows.
8|The king gives Esther Haman's house and makes Mordecai his chief official. A new decree lets the Jews defend themselves.
9|The Jews defeat their enemies on the day set for their destruction. They begin to celebrate the feast of Purim.
10|Mordecai is second to the king and works for his people's good. The chapter ends with Mordecai's explanation of a dream: God has done these things.
11|This added section tells of Mordecai's dream of two dragons fighting and a small spring that becomes a river. It shows God's plan to save his people.
12|Mordecai discovers a plot against the king and reports it. The king rewards him, but Haman is angry and begins to plot against the Jews.
13|This chapter gives the text of the king's letter ordering the Jews destroyed. Mordecai prays to God, saying he would not bow to Haman out of pride, but only because he honors God.
14|Queen Esther prays to the Lord with great fear. She asks God to help her, for she is alone and has no help but him.
15|Esther puts on her royal clothes and goes before the king, but she is afraid. God changes the king's heart, and he welcomes her.
16|This chapter gives the text of the king's second letter, in which he reverses the order against the Jews and says that they are to be treated justly.
""")
